---
description: Plan before big work. Write the test before the implementation.
alwaysApply: true
---

## Planning and TDD

- **Big work gets a plan first.** If a task touches 3+ files or introduces new architecture, use Claude Code's built-in plan mode (or the equivalent planning mode in Cursor) to design before implementing. No skill needed, no token-heavy plugin. Think, write the plan, get user approval, then execute.
- **Every plan opens with `## In short`, right under the title and before Context.** The user decides whether to approve from the top of the plan and can't take in the full design at a glance. Write 2 to 5 one-line bullets in everyday words: what we are changing, why, what they will notice afterwards, and any choice you made that they may want to overturn. Ask open choices through the question tool before presenting the plan; the plan's approval is its own step. File paths, function names and jargon belong in the sections below, not here. In Claude Code a hook blocks ExitPlanMode until this section is there.
- **Every plan also has `## Out of scope` and `## Verification` sections.** Out of scope names what the change leaves alone, so the approval covers its edges. Verification lists the checks and includes one end-to-end check: the command, URL or behavior that proves the whole change works, not only its unit tests.
- **Write the test before the implementation.** For any new function or behavior change: write a failing test first, verify it fails, implement the minimal code to pass it, verify it passes, and repeat that red-green step until the whole task is done. This is not negotiable for logic that has observable correctness criteria.
- **Check anything that runs on every action, or at scale, against real usage.** A guard tested only on invented commands will block the agent's own commit messages and heredocs, and a plan priced on one call misses what seven calls waiting out a cache cost. Before shipping a guard change, replay it over real transcripts and read every sample block in the replay's bounded report.
- **Test count is not a success metric, so never state or imply one.** A large green suite can still miss the one behavior that matters. Judge completion against the definition of done (`.scope.md` when present, otherwise the specific, observable behavior that was asked for), and when reporting status say what behavior was verified, then "tests pass" or "suite is green" with no number in any form (headline, footnote or parenthetical). When you show raw test output, cut the runner's count line.
